592186050948 #edb/ref 1033 561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050948 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7447137e-4148-57e3-9e8c-8ef64742b913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073453 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073453 #edb/ref 1071 "| $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $20$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$ |\n| $25$ | $20$ |\n\nWhat number is missing from the ratio table?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e2f5bec-3573-5f1d-9870-41ac26b1ddf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073453 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}4}$).\n\nWe can make a numerator of $20$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{4 \\cdot 4} &= \\frac{20}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}4}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}16}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}25}$ | ${\\color{red}20}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}16$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073454 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073454 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table below?\n\n| $6$ | $18$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $24$ |\n| $20$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11fac5c9-ca4a-54a6-9f38-3c675411673e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073454 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}6}:{\\color{red}18}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}6}}{{\\color{red}18}}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}6}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}18}$).\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $6$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{6}}\\div 6}{{\\color{red}{18}}\\div 6} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}1}}{{\\color{red}3}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $24$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $8{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 8}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}}{{\\color{red}24}}$\n- To create a numerator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $20{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{1}}\\times 20}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 20} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}20}}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}}$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}6}$ | ${\\color{red}18}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$ | ${\\color{red}24}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}60}$ |\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $60$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073455 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073455 #edb/ref 1071 "Complete the ratio table below.\n\n| $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $10$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $35$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $50$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073455 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2ada4db-c1f0-53b0-9af7-ee2712ae75ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073455 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}5}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}3}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}5}$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $10$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $2{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}}{\\color{red}10}$\n- To create a denominator of $35$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $7{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 7}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 7} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21}}{\\color{red}35}$\n- To create a denominator of $50$, we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $10{:}$\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 10}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 10} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}30}}{\\color{red}50}$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}3}$ | ${\\color{red}5}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21}$ | ${\\color{red}35}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}30}$ | ${\\color{red}50}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073456 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073456 #edb/ref 1071 "Harvey's parents reward him for each hour he spends helping with the housework. The following ratio table shows the number of hours of work Harvey did over the last three weeks and the corresponding rewards.\n\n| Reward (in dollars) | Hours of work |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $3$ |\n| $12$ | $4$ |\n| $15$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}?}$ |\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing numbers in the ratio table?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073456 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1a6757d-3e68-5263-a6c7-d03c73391848" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073456 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}12}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}12}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\nNow we divide the numerator and the denominator by $4$ to find an equivalent ratio:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{12}}\\div 4}{{\\color{red}{4}}\\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate ratios that are equivalent to $3:1$ by finding more equivalent fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 3}{1 \\cdot 3} &= \\frac{9}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 5}{1 \\cdot 5} &= \\frac{15}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| Reward (in dollars) | Hours of work |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$ | $3$ |\n| $12$ | $4$ |\n| $15$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$ |\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $9$ and $5$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073894 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073894 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073894 #edb/ref 1039 "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying: Missing Denominators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073894 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073894 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b9357cc9-e383-5d88-9741-f49f6fbcca99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073895 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073895 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073895 #edb/ref 1039 "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying: Missing Numerators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073895 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073895 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6e3287b0-2ecb-5d83-9596-7943d68f8884" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073896 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186073996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073896 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073896 #edb/ref 1039 "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying and Dividing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073896 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073454 #ed